usage : tty-clock [-iuvsScbtrahDBxn] [-C [0-7]] [-f format] [-d delay] [-a nsdelay] [-T tty] [--copilot] [--copilot-refresh seconds]
    --copilot     Show Copilot usage below the date (P toggles)
    --copilot-refresh seconds  Refresh interval, default 60 (1..86400)
    -s            Show seconds
    -S            Screensaver mode
    -x            Show box
    -c            Set the clock at the center of the terminal
    -C [0-7]      Set the clock color
    -b            Use bold colors
    -t            Set the hour in 12h format
    -u            Use UTC time
    -T tty        Display the clock on the specified terminal
    -r            Do rebound the clock
    -f format     Set the date format
    -n            Don't quit on keypress
    -v            Show tty-clock version
    -i            Show some info about tty-clock
    -h            Show this page
    -D            Hide date
    -B            Enable blinking colon
    -d delay      Set the delay between two redraws of the clock. Default 1s.
    -a nsdelay    Additional delay between two redraws in nanoseconds. Default 0ns.

Copilot usage
-------------
Build with make (requires a C compiler and ncurses development headers).
On Ubuntu/Debian: sudo apt install build-essential libncurses-dev pkg-config

Install GitHub CLI (gh), authenticate with gh auth login on github.com, then run:

    ./tty-clock -c -s --copilot

Below the date, the clock displays a bar filled by the percentage consumed,
with the actual usage on a separate centered line, in the clock's color:

      Copilot [######--------------] 30.5% used
           60,847.1 / 200,000 credits

The bar uses solid background-colored cells when running. Amounts are shown to one
decimal place when needed, with thousands separators. Credit-based accounts
show credits; legacy premium-request accounts show requests. Where available,
actual usage is calculated from entitlement minus quota_remaining, since the
API's integer credits_used can be rounded. Unlimited allowances show usage
without a percentage or denominator when the server supplies it.

Press P to toggle the display. --copilot-refresh 120 changes the refresh interval
to two minutes. Requests run in the background with a 15-second timeout and do
not interrupt the clock or keyboard input. Failed refreshes keep the previous
reading marked stale; unavailable data shows an authentication hint. A missing
premium allowance is treated as unavailable, not as zero usage. With -D the
usage appears directly below the clock. On small terminals the bar shrinks and
long text is clipped safely. Existing screensaver key-exit behavior still applies.

This uses gh api --hostname github.com /copilot_internal/user with the existing
gh credentials. No browser cookies are needed. The endpoint is internal to
GitHub and may change. Only enabling the display starts quota requests.

Verification (after building): python3 tests/test_copilot.py
The tests use a stub gh and pseudo-terminals; they do not access GitHub.
